      whatItSays: "Power is the rate of doing work. It also equals force times velocity when force and velocity are parallel.",
      example: "A car engine exerts 4000 N of driving force at 30 m/s. Power output = 4000 × 30 = 120,000 W = 120 kW. At 60 m/s the same engine would need 240 kW — double the power for double the speed at the same force.",
      deepMeaning: "P = F·v is the instantaneous form: P = dW/dt = F·(ds/dt) = F·v. This is extremely useful in engineering — an engine exerting force F on a vehicle moving at speed v is delivering power Fv. To maintain higher speed, either more force or the same force over more time is required — but power is what the engine is actually rated by. Units: Watts (W) = Joules per second = kg·m²/s³.",
      derivation: "P = dW/dt. Since dW = F·ds, P = F·(ds/dt) = F·v. For constant power and force, W = Pt.",
      whoDiscovered: "James Watt quantified power practically through the unit of horsepower (1 hp ≈ 746 W) in the 1780s to describe steam engine output. The SI unit Watt is named in his honour.",
      whyItMatters: "Power ratings determine the practical capability of engines, motors, electrical systems, and biological organisms. It is the rate at which energy is transformed — the key design parameter for any machine.",
