      whatItSays: "Efficiency is the ratio of useful output energy to total input energy, expressed as a percentage.",
      example: "A petrol car engine converts about 25–30% of the chemical energy in fuel into useful mechanical work. The rest is lost as heat through exhaust and friction. An electric motor can exceed 90% efficiency.",
      deepMeaning: "No real machine is 100% efficient. Energy is always lost to heat through friction, air resistance, electrical resistance, and other dissipative processes. This is a direct consequence of the Second Law of Thermodynamics — entropy always increases in real processes. The maximum theoretical efficiency of a heat engine operating between temperatures T_hot and T_cold is given by the Carnot efficiency: η = 1 − T_cold/T_hot.",
      derivation: "",
