      whatItSays: "When an object rolls without slipping, the contact point has zero instantaneous velocity, linking translational and rotational motion by v = ωr. The total kinetic energy is the sum of translational KE (motion of center of mass) and rotational KE (spinning about center of mass).",
      example: "A solid sphere (I = 2/5 mr²) rolls down a ramp of height h. Energy conservation: mgh = ½mv² + ½(2/5 mr²)(v/r)² = 7/10 mv². So v = √(10gh/7) ≈ 0.845√(2gh) — slower than a frictionless sliding object.",
      deepMeaning: "A hollow cylinder rolls slower down an incline than a solid cylinder because it has a larger fraction of its energy in rotation, leaving less for translation. This is König's theorem in action: the total KE of any rigid body splits cleanly into the KE of the center of mass plus the rotational KE about the center of mass. The rolling constraint v = ωr is the bridge that links the two modes of motion.",
      derivation: "Rolling without slipping means the arc length swept equals the distance traveled: ds = r dθ, so v = ds/dt = r dθ/dt = rω. The total KE follows from König's theorem: KE = ½Mv_cm² + ½I_cm ω².",
      whoDiscovered: "",
      whyItMatters: "Rolling motion governs the dynamics of wheels, cylinders, spheres, and gears in every mechanical system. The race between rolling objects of different shapes is a classic demonstration of how mass distribution affects dynamics.",
      integralForm: "",
      relatedEquations: ["Moments of Inertia", "Kinetic Energy", "Torque", "Newton's Second Law for Rotation"],
      misconception: "Common misconception: the rolling constraint v = ωr applies only when there is no slipping at the contact point. If the surface is frictionless, the object slides rather than rolls, and the translational and rotational motions are decoupled. Also, students sometimes think all the energy goes into translation in rolling motion, but some fraction always goes into rotation — and the fraction depends on the moment of inertia. A hollow cylinder stores a larger fraction in rotation than a solid cylinder, making it slower down an incline.",
      dimensions: "[L][T]⁻¹",
