      relatedEquations: ["Angular Momentum", "Torque", "Newton's Second Law for Rotation"],
      misconception: "Common misconception: angular impulse is zero (and therefore angular momentum is conserved) only when the net external torque is zero — not simply because no external forces act. A force can act on a system and produce zero torque if it is directed toward the axis of rotation (zero moment arm) or if the net torques from all forces cancel. Students sometimes assume that any external force must change angular momentum, forgetting that a force with zero moment arm contributes no torque.",
      dimensions: "[M][L]²[T]⁻¹",
      SI_units: { "τ": "N·m", "Δt": "s", "ΔL": "kg·m²/s" },
      year: 1750,
      branch: "Classical Mechanics",
