      deepMeaning: "An ideal gas is a theoretical model in which molecules have no size and no interactions — they only exchange energy through perfectly elastic collisions. Real gases approach this behaviour at low pressure and high temperature, when molecules are far apart and interactions are negligible. The gas constant R = N_A × k_B connects the macroscopic world (moles, pressure, volume) to the microscopic world (individual molecules). Pressure is an emergent property arising from billions of molecular collisions with container walls per second. Temperature is a measure of the average kinetic energy of those molecules.",
      derivation: "Combines three experimental laws: Boyle's Law (PV = constant at fixed T), Charles's Law (V/T = constant at fixed P), and Avogadro's Law (V ∝ n at fixed P and T). Combining all three gives PV = nRT.",
      whoDiscovered: "The combined law was formulated through the work of Boyle (1662), Charles (1787), Gay-Lussac (1808), and Avogadro (1811). Émile Clapeyron wrote it in its modern combined form in 1834.",
      whyItMatters: "The ideal gas law is the foundation of all thermodynamic calculations involving gases. Engine cycles, atmospheric physics, chemical reactions, astrophysical plasmas — all begin here.",
