      example: "A glass window (k = 1.0 W/m·K), 2 m² area, 4 mm thick, with ΔT = 20°C across it: Q/t = 1.0 × 2 × (20/0.004) = 10,000 W = 10 kW. This illustrates why single-glazing is so thermally inefficient.",
      deepMeaning: "The temperature gradient ΔT/Δx is the driving force for heat flow, exactly as a pressure gradient drives fluid flow and a voltage gradient drives electric current. Heat flows from hot to cold, down the temperature gradient — this is diffusive transport of thermal energy. In differential form: q = −k∇T, where q is the heat flux vector (power per unit area). The negative sign means heat flows in the direction of decreasing temperature. Metals have high k because free electrons carry heat efficiently. Wood and air have low k because energy transfer between molecules is slow.",
