      example: "The Sun (T = 5778 K, R = 6.96 × 10⁸ m, ε ≈ 1): P = 1 × 5.67×10⁻⁸ × 4π(6.96×10⁸)² × 5778⁴ ≈ 3.85 × 10²⁶ W. This matches the measured solar luminosity.",
      deepMeaning: "The T⁴ dependence is remarkably steep — double the temperature and radiated power increases by a factor of 16. This is why the Sun at 5778 K radiates so intensely compared to a warm human body at 310 K. The Stefan-Boltzmann law comes from integrating Planck's blackbody radiation spectrum over all wavelengths — it is a result of quantum statistical mechanics. At room temperature, objects radiate primarily in the infrared. At 6000 K, the peak shifts into visible light.",
      derivation: "Integrate Planck's spectral radiance B(λ,T) = (2hc²/λ⁵) × 1/(e^(hc/λk_BT) − 1) over all wavelengths: P/A = ∫B dλ = σT⁴, where σ = 2π⁵k_B⁴/(15h³c²).",
      whoDiscovered: "Josef Stefan discovered the T⁴ law experimentally in 1879. Ludwig Boltzmann derived it theoretically from thermodynamic principles in 1884.",
      whyItMatters: "Climate science (Earth's energy balance and greenhouse effect), stellar astrophysics (measuring stellar radii and temperatures from luminosity), infrared thermometry, and the blackbody radiation problem that led Planck to quantum mechanics.",
