      deepMeaning: "This law tells you the colour of a star just from its temperature. A cool red star (T ≈ 3000 K) peaks in the infrared and appears red. The Sun (T ≈ 5778 K) peaks in green but appears white because it emits broadly across the visible spectrum. A hot blue star (T ≈ 30,000 K) peaks in the ultraviolet. The historical importance of this law is immense: explaining why intensity does not increase indefinitely at short wavelengths — the ultraviolet catastrophe — was the problem that led Max Planck in 1900 to propose that energy is quantised (E = hf). This was the birth of quantum mechanics.",
      derivation: "Differentiate Planck's spectral radiance B(λ,T) with respect to λ and set to zero. This gives the transcendental equation (5 − x)eˣ = 5 where x = hc/λk_BT, which has the numerical solution x ≈ 4.965. This gives λ_max T = hc/(4.965 k_B) = 2.898 × 10⁻³ m·K.",
