      whatItSays: "The self-inductance of a solenoid with n turns per unit length and volume V is L = μ₀n²V.",
      example: "Solenoid: n = 1000 turns/m, radius 2 cm, length 20 cm. V ≈ 2.51×10⁻⁴ m³. L = 4π×10⁻⁷ × 10⁶ × 2.51×10⁻⁴ ≈ 315 μH. Superconducting MRI magnet: L ≈ 10 H, I = 200 A → stored energy ½LI² = 200 kJ.",
      deepMeaning: "Inductance L = NΦ/I measures flux linkage per unit current. A higher L means more flux for the same current, and more EMF induced when current changes (ε = −L dI/dt). The n² dependence means doubling turns density quadruples inductance. With ferromagnetic core: L = μ₀μᵣn²V, where μᵣ up to ~10,000 — dramatically increasing L. Units: Henry (H) = Wb/A = V·s/A. One henry induces 1 V when current changes at 1 A/s.",
      derivation: "B = μ₀nI. Flux per turn: Φ₁ = μ₀nIA. Total flux linkage for N = nℓ turns: NΦ₁ = μ₀n²Aℓ · I = μ₀n²V · I. Since L = NΦ/I: L = μ₀n²V.",
      whoDiscovered: "Joseph Henry discovered self-induction in 1830. The unit of inductance, the henry, is named in his honour.",
      whyItMatters: "Inductors are fundamental circuit elements used in power supply filters, radio tuners, transformers, impedance matching, and energy storage in switching power supplies. The concept of inductance also governs energy stored in all magnetic fields — including superconducting magnets.",
      integralForm: "L = \\frac{N\\Phi_B}{I} = \\mu_0 n^2 V \\qquad L_{\\text{with core}} = \\mu_0 \\mu_r n^2 V",
      relatedEquations: ["Self-Induced EMF", "Energy Stored in an Inductor", "RL Circuit", "Magnetic Field of a Solenoid"],
      misconception: "Common misconception: inductance L = μ₀n²V depends on the total number of turns N. It depends on n² (turns per unit length squared) times the volume. Doubling N by doubling length leaves n and B unchanged, so L = μ₀n²V doubles (because V doubles) — but the field B stays the same. If you double N by cramming more turns into the same length, n doubles, B doubles, and L quadruples. Students frequently use N where they should use n. Also, inductance depends on the square of n — halving the turns density reduces L by four, not two. The symbol L for inductance can also cause confusion with length L in other formulas.",
      SI_units: { "L": "H", "μ₀": "T·m/A", "n": "m⁻¹", "V": "m³" },
      year: 1831,
