      example: "L = 50 mH, dI/dt = −100 A/s: ε = +5 V — inductor maintains the current. Switching off a relay coil (L = 100 mH, I = 1 A) in 1 μs: ε = 100,000 V — destroys transistors without a flyback diode. Car ignition coil: L ≈ 10 mH, I = 5 A switched in ~1 ms → 10,000–30,000 V across the spark plug gap.",
      deepMeaning: "Electromagnetic inertia: just as F = ma = m dv/dt resists velocity changes, ε = −L dI/dt resists current changes. When current is interrupted, the inductor generates whatever voltage is needed to maintain current — causing dangerous arcing across switch contacts. The negative sign (Lenz's Law): increasing current → opposing EMF; decreasing current → inductor acts as a temporary voltage source. This is why flyback diodes are placed across every inductive load in electronics.",
      derivation: "From Faraday's Law: ε = −dΦ_B/dt = −d(LI)/dt = −L dI/dt (for constant L).",
      whoDiscovered: "Joseph Henry discovered self-induction experimentally in 1830, observing sparks when interrupting current in a coil.",
      whyItMatters: "Governs all transient behaviour in inductive circuits: inductor kickback, boost/buck converter operation in switching power supplies, spark ignition systems, and energy storage in superconducting magnets.",
      integralForm: "\\varepsilon = -L\\frac{dI}{dt} \\quad \\Leftarrow \\quad \\varepsilon = -\\frac{d(N\\Phi_B)}{dt} \\quad \\text{(from Faraday's Law)}",
      relatedEquations: ["Self-Inductance (Solenoid)", "Faraday's Law of Induction", "RL Circuit"],
      misconception: "Common misconception: the back-EMF of an inductor limits current in a DC circuit during steady state. In steady state (constant current), dI/dt = 0 so ε = 0 — the inductor acts as a short circuit (just a wire) at DC. It only opposes changes in current. Students who think an inductor 'resists' DC current in steady state are confusing the transient behaviour (when current is changing) with the long-term steady state (when it is not). The danger with inductors is not their opposition to steady current but their opposition to sudden changes — when a switch disconnects an inductive load, the inductor generates enormous voltages to maintain the current, causing dangerous arcing. This is why inductive loads (motors, relays) always need protection circuits.",
