      deepMeaning: "Compare with capacitor energy ½CV². Capacitors store energy in electric fields; inductors store energy in magnetic fields. The energy density in a magnetic field is u = B²/(2μ₀) — the magnetic analogue of u = ½ε₀E². Together, the total electromagnetic energy density u = ½ε₀E² + B²/(2μ₀) is the energy carried by electromagnetic waves (light). The energy is stored throughout the magnetic field region — not localised in the wire itself.",
      derivation: "Work to build current from 0 to I: W = ∫₀ᴵ L·I dI = ½LI². Energy density: u = B²/(2μ₀); for solenoid B = μ₀nI → U = uV = ½μ₀n²VI² = ½LI².",
      whoDiscovered: "Understood as part of electromagnetic energy theory developed by Maxwell and Heaviside in the 1880s.",
      whyItMatters: "Inductor energy storage is exploited in switching power supplies, superconducting magnetic energy storage (SMES), magnetic forming, and particle accelerator magnets. The energy density B²/(2μ₀) is fundamental — magnetic fields carry energy through space, which is why electromagnetic waves transport energy across the universe.",
      integralForm: "U = \\frac{1}{2}LI^2 \\qquad u = \\frac{B^2}{2\\mu_0} \\quad \\text{(magnetic field energy density, analogous to } \\tfrac{1}{2}\\varepsilon_0 E^2\\text{)}",
      relatedEquations: ["Self-Inductance (Solenoid)", "LC Oscillation Frequency", "Energy Stored in a Capacitor"],
      misconception: "Common misconception: students often treat inductor energy ½LI² as analogous to ½CV² without appreciating the key difference — capacitor energy is stored in the electric field between the plates, while inductor energy is stored in the magnetic field in and around the coil. Neither is 'in the wire' itself. A second misconception: the energy is proportional to I² (not I), so doubling the current quadruples the stored energy — the same non-linear relationship as KE = ½mv². When an inductor is suddenly disconnected, this stored energy must go somewhere; it drives a voltage spike as high as needed to keep the current flowing, which is why inductive switching without protection can destroy electronic components.",
