      deepMeaning: "At t = 0, I = 0 (inductor acts as open circuit — instantaneous current change would require infinite voltage). As t → ∞, I → V/R (inductor acts as short circuit in DC steady state). This is the exact dual of the RC circuit: RC charging → V rises as V(1 − e^(−t/RC)); RL charging → I rises as (V/R)(1 − e^(−Rt/L)). The duality C ↔ L, V ↔ I reflects the deep structural symmetry of circuit theory.",
      derivation: "KVL: V = IR + L(dI/dt). First-order ODE with I(0) = 0: I(t) = (V/R)(1 − e^(−Rt/L)). Inductor voltage: V_L = Ve^(−Rt/L) — starts at V, decays to 0.",
      whoDiscovered: "",
      whyItMatters: "RL time constants determine transient response of all inductive circuits — motor starting, relay switching, transformer behaviour, and current risetime in power electronics. RL circuits act as low-pass filters (Z_L = jωL blocks high frequencies).",
      integralForm: "I(t) = \\frac{V}{R}(1 - e^{-t/\\tau}), \\quad V_L(t) = Ve^{-t/\\tau}, \\quad \\tau = \\frac{L}{R} \\quad \\text{(dual of RC: } \\tau = RC\\text{)}",
      relatedEquations: ["Self-Inductance (Solenoid)", "RC Circuit (Charging)", "Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL)"],
      misconception: "Common misconception: students confuse the RL time constant τ = L/R with the RC time constant τ = RC and mix up which quantity rises exponentially (current in RL, voltage in RC). A deeper error is assuming the inductor limits current in steady state — at DC steady state the inductor is just a wire (zero resistance), so the final current is simply V/R. The inductor only opposes transient changes in current. Also, a larger L or smaller R gives a longer time constant — it takes longer for current to reach its final value. This seems counterintuitive since larger L stores more energy, but the time constant τ = L/R shows that both L and R determine how quickly the system responds.",
