      misconception: "Common misconception: the LC oscillation frequency ω = 1/√(LC) is the natural (resonant) frequency only in an ideal circuit with zero resistance. In a real RLC circuit, the oscillation frequency shifts slightly and the oscillations decay. Students also sometimes confuse the angular frequency ω (rad/s) with the ordinary frequency f (Hz): ω = 2πf — a factor of 2π difference. A further misconception is thinking the capacitor and inductor both carry current simultaneously: during oscillation, when the capacitor is fully charged the current is zero (all energy in E-field), and when the current is maximum the capacitor is fully discharged (all energy in B-field) — they alternate completely, like kinetic and potential energy in a spring-mass system.",
