    misconception: "Common misconception: the Stefan-Boltzmann law P/A = σT⁴ applies to all objects. The law applies only to perfect blackbodies — objects that absorb all incident radiation and emit thermally. Real objects emit less radiation by a factor called emissivity ε (0 ≤ ε ≤ 1): P/A = εσT⁴. Polished metals have ε ≈ 0.02–0.1; matte black surfaces have ε ≈ 0.95–0.99. A human body (ε ≈ 0.97) radiates approximately as a blackbody at T ≈ 310 K, emitting about 500 W of infrared radiation — but also absorbing radiation from the environment. Net heat loss is much smaller. A second misconception: σ is a fundamental constant of nature like c or ℏ. σ = 2π⁵k_B⁴/(15h³c²) — it is fully derived from k_B, h, and c. Since those constants are now exact by SI definition, σ is also exact: σ = 5.670374419... × 10⁻⁸ W/(m²·K⁴). It is a derived constant, not an independent one.",
