    desc: "Governs the spectral lines of hydrogen: the wavenumber of a photon emitted in a transition from level n₂ to n₁ is 1/λ = R∞(1/n₁² − 1/n₂²). Expressible as R∞ = mₑe⁴/(8ε₀²h³c), making it one of the most precisely measured constants in physics.",
    deepMeaning: "R∞ is the wavenumber of the series limit — the highest-energy photon that can be emitted from hydrogen (ionisation from ground state). It is related to the Rydberg energy E_R = R∞hc ≈ 13.6 eV — the ground state binding energy of hydrogen. The subscript ∞ indicates it assumes infinite nuclear mass; the finite proton mass introduces a small correction via the reduced mass. Because R∞ can be measured with extraordinary precision (11 significant figures), it provides one of the most stringent tests of quantum electrodynamics.",
    branch: "Atomic Physics / Quantum Mechanics",
    misconception: "Common misconception: the Rydberg formula 1/λ = R∞(1/n₁² − 1/n₂²) gives the exact wavelengths of hydrogen spectral lines. The subscript ∞ in R∞ indicates infinite nuclear mass — an approximation. For real hydrogen (finite proton mass mₚ), the Rydberg constant is corrected to R_H = R∞ × mₚ/(mₑ + mₚ) ≈ R∞ × 0.9994, via the reduced mass. For hydrogen this correction is ~0.05% — small but measurable. For hydrogen-like helium (He⁺), deuterium, or muonium, the correction is different and must be included. A second misconception: the Rydberg formula applies only to hydrogen. The formula generalises to any one-electron ion: 1/λ = R∞Z²(1/n₁² − 1/n₂²), where Z is the nuclear charge. He⁺ (Z=2) has spectral lines at wavelengths 1/4 of those for hydrogen (same n transitions). Li²⁺ (Z=3) at 1/9. This scaling is why the Rydberg formula was one of the triumphs of the Bohr model and later quantum mechanics.",
