    desc: "The characteristic length scale of atomic physics — the most probable distance of the electron from the proton in the ground state of hydrogen. Defined as a₀ = 4πε₀ℏ²/(mₑe²) = ℏ/(mₑcα). Sets the scale of all atomic dimensions and chemical bond lengths.",
    deepMeaning: "a₀ ≈ 0.529 Å (ångströms) is the natural unit of length in atomic and molecular physics. It emerges from the competition between the electron's kinetic energy (which favours spreading out, lowering momentum) and the electrostatic potential energy (which favours contracting toward the nucleus). The ground-state energy of hydrogen E₁ = −e²/(8πε₀a₀) = −13.6 eV follows directly. In terms of fundamental constants: a₀ = ℏ/(αmₑc), where α is the fine structure constant — showing that the Bohr radius is the electron's Compton wavelength divided by 2πα.",
    branch: "Atomic Physics / Quantum Mechanics",
    misconception: "Common misconception: a₀ ≈ 0.529 Å is the radius of the electron's orbit in hydrogen. In Bohr's model (1913), a₀ is the radius of the ground-state circular orbit. In quantum mechanics, the electron has no definite orbit — it is described by a probability distribution ψ. The 1s wavefunction has its maximum probability density |ψ|² at the origin (the nucleus), not at a₀. However, the radial probability distribution P(r) = 4πr²|ψ|² (which accounts for shell volume) peaks at r = a₀. So a₀ is the most probable distance from the nucleus, not the average distance (which is ⟨r⟩ = 3a₀/2 for the ground state). A second misconception: a₀ is a fixed length that doesn't depend on what kind of hydrogen-like atom it is. For hydrogen-like ions with nuclear charge Z, the effective Bohr radius is a₀/Z — the electron is pulled closer by the stronger nuclear charge. For muonic hydrogen (a muon replacing the electron, mass 207mₑ), the Bohr radius is a₀/207 ≈ 2.56 × 10⁻¹³ m — inside the proton's charge radius, making muonic hydrogen sensitive to nuclear structure.",
