    desc: "The dimensionless coupling constant of electromagnetism: α = e²/(4πε₀ℏc) ≈ 1/137.036. One of the deepest unexplained numbers in physics — its value governs the structure of atoms, the periodic table, chemistry, and the nature of matter itself.",
    deepMeaning: "α is dimensionless — it has the same value in every system of units, making it a truly fundamental pure number that nature has chosen. In quantum electrodynamics, α is the probability amplitude for an electron to emit or absorb a photon; its smallness (≈ 1/137) is why perturbation theory works in QED and why the series expansion in powers of α converges. If α were much larger, atoms would collapse (electrons would spiral into nuclei). If α were much smaller, chemistry would not exist as we know it — atoms would barely interact. Richard Feynman called α 'one of the greatest damn mysteries of physics' — no theory explains why it is 1/137 rather than any other value.",
    branch: "Electromagnetism / Quantum Field Theory",
    misconception: "Common misconception: α = 1/137.036 is a fixed, universal number — the same at all energies. α 'runs' with energy due to vacuum polarisation in quantum electrodynamics. At low energies (e.g. in atomic physics), α ≈ 1/137.036. At the Z boson mass scale (~91 GeV), α ≈ 1/128. This running of the coupling constant is a fundamental prediction of quantum field theory, confirmed experimentally at LEP. The value '1/137' is the infrared (low-energy) fixed point. A second misconception: the closeness of α to 1/137 (an integer fraction) has deep significance and must be explainable by a simple theory. The actual value is α⁻¹ ≈ 137.035999084 — not exactly 137, and no accepted theory derives it from simpler numbers. Eddington's numerological attempts to derive 1/136 (later 1/137) from first principles are not accepted physics. The value of α is one of the most fundamental unexplained numbers in physics, and any theoretical framework that could predict it from first principles would be a revolutionary advance.",
